<% content_for :title, "Remindly — free reminder phone calls for a parent living on their own" %>
<%#
  This sentence is what Google prints under the result, which makes it the most
  widely read claim on the site — so it is held to the same standard as the FAQ:
  Remindly knows a dose was *marked done* on the device, and cannot know whether
  a tablet was swallowed. "Taken" implies a verification this product does not
  perform, and medication is the wrong place to be loose about it.

  "Missed" is the same overclaim pointed the other way — it describes a dose
  rather than an unmarked reminder, and the two come apart exactly when someone
  takes their tablets and forgets to press the button.

  The telephone now opens the sentence rather than arriving in a subordinate
  clause at the end of it. A reader whose parent owns no tablet had to get
  three clauses in before learning this was for them, and that reader is most
  of the market: the tablet is the second option in almost every house, and it
  was being sold as the first.
%>
<% content_for :description, "Remindly telephones a parent living on their own and says the reminder out loud — a telephone call to the phone already in the house, or spoken from a tablet if there is one. They press one key. You set the reminders up from wherever you are, and for medication Remindly emails you when a dose is marked done, or when the time passes and nobody marks it. Free to use." %>

<%#
  Declares the product to search engines: what it is, who it is for, and that it
  costs nothing. The price matters most — "free" in an offer is what lets a
  result carry that label, and it is the single fact most likely to decide
  whether someone comparing options clicks through.
%>
<% content_for :structured_data, json_ld(
     "@context" => "https://schema.org",
     "@type" => "SoftwareApplication",
     "name" => "Remindly",
     "applicationCategory" => "HealthApplication",
     "operatingSystem" => "Any device with a web browser",
     "url" => canonical_host,
     "description" => content_for(:description),
     "offers" => {
       "@type" => "Offer",
       "price" => "0",
       "priceCurrency" => "USD"
     },
     "audience" => {
       "@type" => "Audience",
       "audienceType" => "Family caregivers and older adults living independently"
     }
   ) %>

<%#
  "Caring for a parent from a distance" described the reader's situation, which
  they already know they are in. It said nothing about what this is, and the
  lead beneath it took sixty words and three em-dashed clauses to get there.

  What replaces it is a category claim, because the category is the thing being
  got wrong: everyone arriving here assumes a reminder product means an app,
  and an app is the reason they have already given up on two others. Naming the
  telephone in the first four words disqualifies nobody and rescues the house
  with no tablet in it.
%>
<h1>A phone call, not another app to learn</h1>

<p class="lead">
  Remindly rings their own telephone at the right time and says the reminder out
  loud — the morning tablets, Thursday's appointment, a glass of water. They
  press one key. You get an email saying what happened, so checking in does not
  have to mean ringing to ask.
</p>

<%#
  One action, named by its outcome. "Get started" said nothing about what you
  would be starting, and sat beside a nav "Sign in" pointing at the same place —
  three visible options for two things. The secondary is deliberately worded as
  a detour rather than an alternative, so it reads as "not yet" instead of "or".
%>
<div class="actions">
  <a class="btn btn-primary" href="<%= login_path %>">Set up reminders for my parent — free</a>
  <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="<%= how_to_path %>">See how it works first</a>
</div>

<%#
  The three things a caregiver braces for on a free product. An earlier version
  led with "no limit on how many reminders", which is a worry nobody arrives
  with; it survived as a tail clause until it was dropped altogether.

  Dropped rather than reworded, and worth recording why. It persuaded nobody --
  the argument against leading with it is the argument against keeping it -- and
  a cap on reminders is the most ordinary way this could one day be charged for.
  A sentence that wins no one over is a bad thing to have promised.

  What is left is deliberately narrower and all of it is meant. "No trial that
  quietly ends" is the load-bearing one: it is read by somebody about to wire
  their mother's morning dose to this, and rewriting it later does not take it
  back from them.
%>
<p class="free-note">
  <strong>Free while in beta — no card, no ads, no sales calls.</strong> No
  trial that quietly ends: if charging ever becomes necessary, you will be told
  first.
</p>

<%#
  The practical objection, answered before anything else: what device, is there
  an app, what do I actually have to do. These are the same three steps that
  used to sit under "What it looks like at yours", roughly five hundred words
  down — the content was right, its position was not.

  There is deliberately no claim about how long setup takes. Nobody has timed
  it, and inventing a number on the page a caregiver trusts with their parent's
  medication is not a copy decision. Measure it, then say it here.

  Four steps now rather than three, and each named by who acts. The three said
  "you create", "your parent hears", "you see" — which skipped the step the
  reader is most unsure about, the one where an eighty-year-old is expected to
  do something. Making "they press one key" its own numbered step is the point:
  it is a step, it is small, and hiding it inside another one made it look like
  something being glossed over.
%>
<section class="setup">
  <h2>Works with what they already have — a telephone, or a tablet if there is one</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>You schedule it</strong> — the morning tablets, an appointment, a
      walk after lunch — from your own phone or computer, wherever you are.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Remindly calls</strong> — their own telephone rings at the right
      time and says the reminder out loud. Nothing to install, and no app store
      account. If there is a tablet or a computer in the house, Remindly can
      speak from that instead.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>They press one key</strong> — 1 for done, 2 to be reminded again
      shortly. On a screen it is one big button instead. Never more than one
      thing to press, and nothing to read.
    </li>
    <li>
      <%# Scoped to medication on purpose. Acknowledged and missed emails go to
          caregivers opted into the reminder's category, and medication is the
          only one on by default -- see ReminderNotificationService. Promising
          this for every reminder would be promising the firehose a caregiver
          has to ask for. %>
      <strong>You are told</strong> — for medication, an email when it is marked
      done, and another if the time passes and nobody marks it.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p class="setup-foot">
    Any device with a web browser and a speaker will do — an old tablet, a
    laptop, the computer already on the desk. Signing in is a link sent by
    email, so there is no password for anyone to remember.
  </p>

  <%# The limits, in the same panel as the promise. The whole case for the calls
      belongs on /how_to and the elderly parents page; what belongs here is
      everything that would feel like a bait-and-switch if it were found later.
      Leaving the hours and the opt-out for the other page would make this the
      reassuring version and that one the true one. %>
  <p class="setup-foot">
    <strong>The limits, before you build anything on this.</strong> Calls are
    made between 8am and 9pm at their end. Nobody is telephoned without agreeing
    first — the opening call only asks, and never asks for personal details —
    and pressing 9 on any call stops the reminder calls.
    <a href="<%= how_to_path %>">How the calls work</a>.
  </p>
</section>

<%#
  The single biggest unknown for somebody deciding, and until now the page
  answered it nowhere: what does an automated call to my mother actually sound
  like. Every quoted line here is the script in config/locales/voice.en.yml,
  word for word, including the screening line that plays first on every call.
  spec/requests/home_call_transcript_spec.rb holds them together — if the
  script is reworded and this is not, that spec fails, which is the only thing
  standing between this section and becoming a plausible fiction.
%>
<section class="transcript" aria-labelledby="transcript-heading">
  <h2 id="transcript-heading">What the call actually sounds like</h2>
  <p class="transcript-when">Eight in the morning. Her own telephone rings.</p>

  <ol class="lines">
    <li>
      <span class="who">Remindly</span>
      <span class="said">“This is Remindly, with a reminder. Press 1 to hear it.”</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="who">Margaret</span>
      <span class="did">presses 1</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="who">Remindly</span>
      <span class="said">“Hello Margaret. This is Remindly, with your reminder.
      Take your morning medication. Press 1 if you have done it. Press 2 to be
      reminded again in 10 minutes. Press 9 to stop these calls.”</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="who">Margaret</span>
      <span class="did">presses 1</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="who">You</span>
      <span class="did">an email, a minute later: her morning medication was
      marked done at 8:01.</span>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p class="transcript-foot">
    That is the whole of it. It names Remindly before it asks for anything,
    because an automated voice telling an older person to press a key is the
    shape of the calls this age group is warned about — and being recognisable
    in the first three seconds is the only defence that survives a mobile
    network stripping the caller ID.
  </p>
</section>

<%#
  The question the transcript raises and does not answer, and the one a
  caregiver is really buying against: she did not pick up, now what. Numbers
  rather than adjectives, because "we keep trying" is what every product on
  this shelf says. Three attempts and five minutes are TelnyxCall::MAX_ATTEMPTS
  and RETRY_AFTER; a spec pins them so the copy cannot drift from the code.

  The second paragraph is scoped to time-critical reminders because that is
  what ReminderNotificationService.notify_unanswered checks. Saying it
  unqualified would promise every caregiver an email on every silent hydration
  call, which is neither what happens nor what anybody wants.
%>
<h2>A call nobody answers does not just vanish</h2>

<p>
  If the telephone rings out, Remindly tries again — up to three times, five
  minutes apart, and only inside the hours above. A voicemail cannot press a
  key, so an answering machine does not count as an answer and the reminder is
  never left on one.
</p>

<p>
  When you have marked a reminder as one where <strong>being late matters</strong>,
  you are emailed on every attempt that goes unanswered, with how many are left
  — not once at the end of the day, and not only after the last try. That is
  the whole reason that setting exists.
</p>

<%#
  Real screenshots of the running app, not mockups — width and height are the
  file's own dimensions so the browser reserves the space and the text below
  does not jump when the image arrives.

  The care receiver's screen comes first. The buyer is the adult child, but the
  objection that stops them is "my mother will never use this", and that is
  answered by a picture rather than a paragraph.

  Still lazy-loaded, though. Moving it up the document did not move it above the
  fold: the hero, the buttons, the free note and the setup panel run past the
  first screen on a laptop and well past it on a phone, so this is roughly 40KB
  fetched eagerly to compete with content the visitor can actually see.
%>
<figure class="shot">
  <img src="/screenshot-voice-reminders.webp"
       width="1400" height="1179" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
       alt="The Remindly screen a care receiver sees: a heading reading My Reminders, a note that there are six reminders today, then each reminder as a large card — Take your morning medication at 8:00 AM with the note two white tablets with breakfast, and Have a glass of water at 10:30 AM — each with a big green Done button and a Snooze button beside it.">
  <figcaption>
    <strong>No app to learn. Just a familiar screen that speaks when it's time.</strong>
    Everything is large, and there are only ever two things to press.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>If there is a tablet in the house</h2>

<p>
  A tablet or computer sits somewhere they already spend time — the kitchen
  table, beside a favourite chair — with Remindly open on the screen, the way a
  clock sits on a shelf. When a reminder is due, it speaks:
  <em>"Take your morning medication."</em>
</p>

<p>
  The screen lists the day's reminders in large type, each with two buttons:
  <strong>Done</strong> and <strong>Snooze</strong>. Nothing to install, no
  password to remember, no menus to get lost in.
</p>

<h2>What it looks like at yours</h2>

<p>
  You see the whole day at a glance from your own dashboard. For medication,
  Remindly emails you when a dose is <strong>marked done</strong> on their
  device, and again if the time passes and it has not been — so checking in does
  not have to mean ringing to ask whether they remembered.
</p>

<div class="note">
  <p>
    <strong>What "done" means, and what it doesn't.</strong> Remindly records
    that someone pressed <strong>Done</strong> on the reminder. That is a good
    signal and it is not proof: it cannot tell you a tablet was actually
    swallowed, and it is not a substitute for a pill dispenser, a carer or
    medical advice. If a missed dose would be dangerous, Remindly should not be
    the only thing standing between your parent and that dose.
  </p>
</div>

<p>
  Alongside the reminders that repeat, there is a shared list of the one-off
  things — a prescription to collect, an appointment on Thursday, the grocery
  delivery someone needs to be in for. If more than one of you is helping, you
  can each take what you can and see what the others have picked up.
</p>

<figure class="shot">
  <img src="/screenshot-tasks.webp"
       width="1400" height="744" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
       alt="The caregiver's task list for Margaret Ellis, showing five tasks with dates: ring about the boiler service marked Completed, collect repeat prescription marked High priority and Pending, a cardiology appointment marked High and Assigned, a grocery delivery marked Medium and Assigned, and change the bed linen marked Low and Pending — each showing who it is assigned to, or Unassigned.">
  <figcaption>What you see. Who is doing what, what is still unclaimed, and what has already been dealt with.</figcaption>
</figure>

<div class="note">
  <p>
    <strong>Two things worth knowing before you set this up.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    Remindly speaks through the page, so that page needs to stay open on their
    device — think of it as leaving a clock on the counter rather than an app you
    open when you need it. Close the tab and nothing is spoken.
  </p>
  <p>
    And browsers will not play sound until someone has touched the screen, so on
    an iPad Remindly asks for a single tap after it starts up. It remembers
    anything it could not say and speaks it once you do.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>What it costs</h2>

<p>
  Nothing, while it is in beta — every reminder, every caregiver you link, every
  email it sends you. There is no card to enter and no trial that quietly ends.
  If charging ever becomes necessary, anyone already using Remindly will be told
  first, well before anything changes.
</p>

<p>
  It is early software, and being useful to people matters more right now than
  being paid for. If that ever changes, anyone already using Remindly will hear
  it from us first, and well before it does.
</p>

<%#
  The FAQ was reachable from the homepage only through one link in the last
  paragraph and the footer — both below everything — despite being the page
  that carries the long-tail search content and the FAQPage graph. Someone
  reading about cost is exactly the person wondering what the catch is, so this
  is where the link belongs.
%>
<p>
  If that sounds like there must be a catch,
  <a href="<%= faq_path %>">the questions page says plainly why there isn't</a> —
  along with what Remindly deliberately does not do.
</p>

<%#
  The one internal link to the landing page, and it is here rather than higher
  up on purpose: someone still reading at this point is deciding, and that page
  is written for deciding. It also has to be linked from somewhere — a page
  reachable only through the sitemap is one a search engine has no reason to
  think matters.

  It sits above "Getting started" rather than at the foot, though. It used to be
  the last thing on the page, which meant the page closed by suggesting the
  reader go and look at something else.
%>
<p>
  Still comparing options? What a
  <a href="<%= reminder_app_for_elderly_parents_path %>">reminder app for an
  elderly parent</a> actually has to do is a shorter list than it looks, and
  worth checking against anything else you are considering.
</p>

<h2>Getting started</h2>

<p>
  Enter your email on the <a href="<%= login_path %>">sign-in page</a> and
  Remindly sends you a link. There is no password to choose or remember, and
  nothing to install.
</p>

<p>
  The first time you sign in, Remindly asks one question — whether you are setting
  it up for someone else or will be receiving the reminders yourself — and takes
  you straight in. No waiting, and you can change that answer later from your
  profile.
</p>

<%#
  The sentence the homepage could not honestly print until this month.

  "You create the reminders" was the promise at the top of this page while the
  person receiving them still had to sign up, find an email and read a long code
  down a telephone before any of it worked. Saying so here, in the section
  someone reads once they have decided to try it, is the point of having built
  it — and the consent line belongs in the same breath, because a reader whose
  next thought is "so I can set this up on my mother without telling her" should
  meet the answer immediately rather than discover it later.
%>

<p>
  Then you set up the person you care for — their name and their timezone, and
  that is the whole form. <strong>They need no email address, no password and no
  account.</strong> Write their reminders straight away, and hand over access
  either by opening the link on their tablet and bookmarking it, or by reading
  them six numbers down the telephone to type in.
</p>

<p>
  Nothing is spoken and you see nothing about their day until they agree, on
  their own screen, which names you when it asks. If they say no, everything you
  set up is deleted. That is deliberate: a reminder system arranged for somebody
  without their knowledge is not a thing Remindly will help you build.
</p>

<p>
  The <a href="<%= how_to_path %>">guide</a> walks through setting up a first
  reminder once you are in, and the
  <a href="<%= faq_path %>">questions page</a> covers what this looks like in
  practice — including what Remindly deliberately does not do.
</p>

<%#
  A page this long ends, as it did, on a link to a comparison page — which asks
  the reader to go and keep shopping. What it needed was the moment being sold,
  put in a specific tomorrow rather than in the abstract, and the same button
  from the top within reach of somebody who has now read everything.

  Not inside .actions: a spec counts the primary buttons in that container so
  the hero keeps offering exactly one decision, and this must not satisfy it.
%>
<h2>Tomorrow at eight, you could already know</h2>

<p>
  Her telephone rings, she presses 1, and the email is with you before the
  kettle has boiled. Nothing for her to learn, nothing for you to install, and
  nothing to pay.
</p>

<div class="actions-end">
  <a class="btn btn-primary" href="<%= login_path %>">Set up reminders for my parent — free</a>
</div>

